{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores in silicon-carbon anodes as stress-regulation units to mitigate lithiation-induced deformation and interphase degradation. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the material redirects expansion stress internally, suppresses electrode swelling, and improves Li+ kinetics. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 capacity, 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, reducing thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores in heteroatom-doped carbon hosts redirect expansion stress internally, suppressing electrode swelling",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composites achieve 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity and 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency",
    "The design reduces thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles and enables scalable production for 2 Ah NCM811 pouch cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "Material design and synthesis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore stress regulation as a scalable structural strategy for practical low-expansion silicon-carbon anodes, addressing key mechanical and electrochemical instability challenges in high-energy lithium-ion batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study integrated single-cell epigenomic data from 2.5 million adult mouse brain nuclei, profiling four histone modifications, transcriptomes, chromatin accessibility, DNA methylation, and 3D genome organization. The resulting atlas identified 100+ brain cell subclasses and defined active, primed, and repressive chromatin states across 81% of the genome. Active states with combinatorial histone marks better predict functional enhancers than accessibility alone, while H3K27me3 and H3K9me3 mark distinct repression mechanisms. The resource enables deep learning models for predicting epigenomic features and gene expression from DNA sequences.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The atlas spans over 100 brain cell subclasses using integrated epigenomic profiling",
    "Active chromatin states defined by combinatorial histone modifications better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone",
    "H3K27me3- and H3K9me3-marked chromatin states delineate distinct modes of cell-type-specific gene repression"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-cell epigenomic profiling",
  "scientific_significance": "This resource provides a comprehensive framework for annotating the brain regulatory genome and interpreting non-coding disease risk variants through multimodal epigenomic integration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether trans-splicing of pre-mRNAs generates functional proteins in mammals, focusing on inflammatory macrophages. Researchers identified chimeric RNAs produced during macrophage polarization, which may influence cytokine release. The findings suggest trans-splicing could contribute to mammalian inflammatory responses, though the exact mechanisms remain unclear.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Chimeric RNAs were generated in macrophages polarized to an inflammatory state",
    "These chimeric RNAs potentially impact cytokine release pathways",
    "Trans-splicing occurs in mammalian inflammatory responses, challenging previous assumptions about its rarity"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that trans-splicing events can produce functional proteins in mammalian immune cells, expanding understanding of post-transcriptional mechanisms regulating inflammatory responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how WEE1 kinase regulates cell cycle progression from quiescence by inhibiting APC/CCDH1 activity. The research demonstrates that WEE1 phosphorylates CDH1 (FZR1) to disrupt its interaction with APC/C, delaying S phase entry and causing p21-dependent G2 arrest in WEE1-deficient cells. Reduced APC/CCDH1 or p21 expression enhances sensitivity to WEE1 inhibitors, suggesting a mechanism for WEE1 inhibitor resistance in TP53-wild-type tumors.",
  "key_findings": [
    "WEE1 phosphorylates CDH1 (FZR1) to inhibit APC/CCDH1-mediated cyclin degradation",
    "WEE1 loss delays S phase entry and induces p21-dependent G2 arrest",
    "APC/CCDH1 or p21 downregulation increases sensitivity to WEE1 inhibitors"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro cell-based study",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies a novel WEE1-APC/CCDH1 regulatory axis that explains WEE1 inhibitor efficacy in TP53-wild-type tumors and provides a mechanistic basis for overcoming clinical resistance to these therapies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how protein kinase A (PKA) signaling regulates group 3 innate lymphoid cell (ILC3) homeostasis and IL-23-driven gut inflammation. Using a single-cell atlas, researchers found that prostaglandin activates PKA to promote tissue-protective functions, while IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors like PRKAR1A. Deletion of Prkar1a reduced ILC3s and increased infection susceptibility in mice. ILC3s from healthy humans express PRKAR1A, which is downregulated by IL-23 or inflammatory bowel disease, indicating PKA signaling's critical role in maintaining gut immune balance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors (PRKAR1A) in ILC3s",
    "Prkar1a deletion reduced ILC3 numbers and increased enteric infection susceptibility in mice",
    "ILC3s from healthy humans express PRKAR1A, downregulated by IL-23 or inflammatory bowel disease"
  ],
  "study_type": "single-cell atlas",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes PKA signaling as a key regulator of ILC3 homeostasis, revealing how IL-23 disrupts this balance to drive intestinal inflammation through PRKAR1A modulation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study uses spatiotemporal intensity-cycling microscopy to investigate many-body interactions and correlated transport in van der Waals semiconductors. The approach enables selective imaging of single-particle and multi-pusing transport, revealing exciton-exciton annihilation dynamics, dielectric disorder mapping, and polaronic Mott transitions. The findings provide a high-resolution tool for visualizing quantum correlations in material systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Clean isolation of single-particle charge transport with high signal-to-noise ratio",
    "Spatiotemporal tracking of exciton-exciton annihilation",
    "Imaging of polaronic Mott transitions and correlated transport in superatomic solids"
  ],
  "study_type": "Spatiotemporal intensity-cycling microscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "The method enables direct visualization of many-body quantum correlations in van der Waals semiconductors, advancing understanding of how interactions govern material functionality."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how fiscal discipline and physical resilience can be aligned to avoid competition between economic stability and disaster preparedness. The research proposes that strategic reforms could redirect resources to enhance both areas simultaneously, potentially improving societal resilience without compromising fiscal health. The findings suggest a framework for integrating fiscal policies with disaster risk management.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Reforms can shift fiscal discipline and resilience investment from competing to reinforcing forces",
    "Strategic resource allocation enhances both economic stability and disaster preparedness",
    "Integrated fiscal policies improve societal resilience without compromising fiscal health"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances understanding of policy integration by demonstrating how fiscal discipline and disaster resilience can be mutually reinforcing, offering a novel approach to balancing economic stability with risk mitigation in resource-constrained systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the molecular mechanisms triggering lethal gut graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) post-transplantation. Using mouse models, researchers found that early IFN-γ-driven MHC class II expression on intestinal epithelial cells promotes cytolytic TH1 cell differentiation and intestinal stem cell depletion, leading to GVHD pathogenesis through a feed-forward IFN-γ-MHCII axis.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IFN-γ-dependent induction of MHC class II on intestinal epithelial cells within 3 days post-transplantation",
    "Perforin/granzyme B-expressing cytolytic donor TH1 cells expand locally in the ileum",
    "Intestinal stem cells are progressively depleted via MHCII-dependent cytolytic mechanisms"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "The study reveals a critical feed-forward IFN-γ-MHCII axis that initiates cytolytic TH1-mediated gut damage in early GVHD, providing mechanistic insight into a key pathway for intestinal injury in allogeneic transplantation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates kinetic-environmental coupling at buried interfaces in ambient flexible perovskite solar cells, which limits device performance. Using dual-side in situ photoluminescence, researchers discovered simultaneous nucleation at gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during processing. They found outward solvent evaporation delays α-phase conversion at the buried interface, causing kinetic mismatch that worsens moisture damage. To resolve this, a self-segregating molecule (NFA) was designed to synchronize crystallization, passivate defects, and form a hydrophobic barrier. This approach achieved record power-conversion efficiencies of 24.84% in small cells and 21.46% in mini-modules, maintaining 96.26% efficiency after 1000h damp aging at 85±5% RH.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Simultaneous nucleation occurs at both gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during antisolvent processing",
    "Outward solvent evaporation delays α-phase conversion at the buried interface, exacerbating moisture damage",
    "Self-segregating NFA molecule synchronizes crystallization, passivates defects, and forms a hydrophobic barrier"
  ],
  "study_type": "In situ photoluminescence analysis with antisolvent-assisted solution processing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes self-segregated interfacial regulation as a general strategy to overcome kinetic-environmental trade-offs in ambient flexible perovskite solar cells."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how Fc-active anti-TIGIT and anti-PD-L1 combination therapy promotes non-exhausted CD8+ T cell differentiation. It identified conventional dendritic cells (cDCs) as essential for efficacy, revealing an FcγR+ cDC1-like activation state with elevated costimulatory ligands. Therapy induced CD8+ T cells to express CD28 and upregulate CD226, with CD28 signaling and cDC-derived PVR driving non-exhausted T cell differentiation via an IFN-I-amplified cascade.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Fc-active anti-TIGIT and anti-PD-L1 combination therapy induces non-exhausted CD8+ T cell differentiation",
    "Conventional dendritic cells (cDCs) are required for therapeutic efficacy",
    "CD28 signaling and cDC-derived PVR promote non-exhausted T cell differentiation through an IFN-I-amplified cascade"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mechanistic study",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings elucidate a sequential costimulatory cascade initiated by FcγR-expressing dendritic cells that drives non-exhausted T cell differentiation, providing mechanistic insights into combination immunotherapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates zeolite-catalysed olefin carbonylation by leveraging spatial confinement in FER zeolite. Using a high CO/olefin ratio (360), it achieves selective production of primary and secondary carboxylic acids from ethylene and propylene. Selectivity for propanoic acid (80% over 100 h) and isobutyric acid (95% over 750 h) demonstrates the method's efficacy under controlled conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "FER zeolite with CO/olefin ratio 360 produces primary and secondary carboxylic acids from olefins",
    "Ethylene carbonylation achieves 80% selectivity to propanoic acid over 100 hours",
    "Propylene carbonylation achieves 95% selectivity to isobutyric acid over 750 hours"
  ],
  "study_type": "Heterogeneous catalysis using zeolite",
  "scientific_significance": "The work establishes spatial confinement in zeolites as a key factor for enabling selective carbonylation of olefins to primary and secondary carboxylic acids, advancing heterogeneous catalysis for carbonylation reactions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates reprogramming tumour-associated macrophages in malignant ascites using CAR-engineered macrophages and TREM2 degradation via lipid nanoparticles. The approach targets TREM2+ macrophages with mRNA-loaded nanoparticles incorporating a TREM2 ligand to enhance internalization and degradation. Results show inhibited AKT signaling, improved CD8+ T cell responses, reduced immunosuppression, and tumor growth suppression in mice when combined with PD-1 blockade.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Lipid nanoparticles with TREM2 ligand enhance targeting and degradation of TREM2+ macrophages",
    "Inhibition of AKT signaling reprograms macrophages to an antitumour phenotype",
    "Combined therapy with PD-1 blockade suppresses tumour growth and extends survival in mice"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo mouse models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a targeted immunotherapeutic strategy to overcome macrophage-mediated immunosuppression in peritoneal metastasis, providing a novel mechanism for reprogramming tumour-associated macrophages to enhance antitumour immunity."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how BAX and BAK activation drives mitochondrial DNA efflux and caspase-independent cell death. Researchers purified a multiprotein complex containing BAX, BAK, BH3-only proteins, VDAC1, VDAC2, and prohibitins that releases mtDNA. The complex formation triggers VDAC closure, calcium influx, and mtDNA efflux, with loss of VDAC1/2 or prohibitins blocking efflux without impairing mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization.",
  "key_findings": [
    "BAX-BAK activation forms a multiprotein complex releasing mtDNA",
    "VDAC1 and VDAC2 closure occurs during BAX-BAK activation",
    "Loss of VDAC1, VDAC2, or prohibitins prevents mtDNA efflux without affecting MOMP"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro purification of macromolecular complexes",
  "scientific_significance": "The study reveals distinct molecular mechanisms for cytochrome c versus mtDNA efflux during apoptosis, clarifying how BAX-BAK complexes regulate different death pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the molecular mechanisms maintaining CD4 T cell precursors during chronic viral infections using the LCMV model. It identifies CXCR5+TCF-1+ CD4 T cells as precursors in secondary lymphoid organs that transition into terminally differentiated cells with TFH and TH1 features. The research demonstrates that TOX and PD-1 are essential for preserving these precursors, as their absence reduces precursor numbers and impairs differentiation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CXCR5+TCF-1+ CD4 T cells serve as precursors for CD4 populations in chronic infections",
    "These precursors express TOX and PD-1, which maintain their population",
    "Absence of TOX or PD-1 reduces precursor numbers and impairs differentiation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model using LCMV infection",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a hierarchical framework for CD4 T cell maintenance in chronic infection, revealing shared molecular mechanisms that sustain both exhausted CD4 and CD8 T cell responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated ultrasound-activated hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks (HDTO) to overcome epigenetic silencing of gasdermin E (GSDME) in cancer immunotherapy. The HDTO nanoplatform, engineered with ROS-responsive decitabine, releases demethylating agents upon ultrasound to restore GSDME expression and induce caspase-3-mediated pyroptosis. In vivo results showed enhanced immunogenic cell death, tumor microenvironment remodeling, and significant antitumor efficacy through monocyte/neutrophil expansion.",
  "key_findings": [
    "US-activated HDTO nanoplatform restores GSDME expression via DFNA5 demethylation",
    "Caspase-3 activation triggers pyroptosis to enhance immunogenic cell death",
    "In vivo treatment reduced tumor growth and metastasis with monocyte/neutrophil expansion"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo and in vitro studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a synergistic strategy for epigenetic reprogramming and pyroptosis induction to overcome immunotherapy resistance, advancing cancer treatment through enhanced antitumor immunity."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether granzyme K (GzmK) drives age-related tissue decline independently of immune aging. Using young and aged GzmK-deficient mice, researchers found GzmK is critical for tissue remodeling in kidneys and livers but dispensable for immune aging hallmarks. Deficiency reduced organ aging markers like fibrosis and complement activation without affecting immune dysfunction.",
  "key_findings": [
    "GzmK deficiency attenuated age-related organ aging in kidneys and livers",
    "Reduced complement deposition in kidneys and improved liver function with decreased fibrosis",
    "GzmK drives tissue-specific aging without contributing to immune aging phenotypes"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse models",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish granzyme K as a tissue-specific mediator of organismal aging, decoupling immune and tissue aging processes and revealing potential targets for age-related tissue decline interventions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how cold temperatures extend lifespan and proteostasis in C. elegans by identifying a metabolite-driven signaling pathway. Cold upregulates the biopterin-synthesis enzyme CAT-4, increasing dihydrobiopterin (BH2) production. BH2 is transported to distal tissues where it converts to tetrahydrobio:biopterin (BH4), enhancing tyrosine synthesis and longevity while preventing protein aggregation. The findings suggest BH2 as a key cold-induced metabolite promoting systemic proteostasis and longevity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cold temperatures upregulate CAT-4 to increase dihydrobiopterin (BH2) production",
    "BH2 is transported to distal tissues and converted to BH4, promoting tyrosine synthesis and lifespan extension",
    "BH2 supplementation reduces aggregation of Huntington's disease and ALS-associated proteins in human cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo model using C. elegans",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies a conserved cold-induced metabolite pathway that enhances systemic proteostasis and longevity, providing mechanistic insights into temperature-mediated healthspan extension across species."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how proximal redox coupling stabilizes atomically precise catalysts during water electrolysis. Using in situ X-ray absorption spectroscopy, researchers designed Ru-O-Cr motifs that enable oxidation damping by synchronizing attenuated Ru oxidation with reversible Cr redox changes. The catalysts demonstrated significantly reduced degradation rates compared to conventional Ru-based systems under acidic conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "In situ X-ray absorption spectroscopy revealed attenuated Ru oxidation synchronized with reversible Cr redox evolution",
    "Proximal Ru-O-Cr motifs increased oxidation resistance by 55.7-fold versus conventional SACs",
    "Catalysts showed 6.8-fold and 14.4-fold lower degradation rates than Ru SACs and commercial RuO2 at 1 A cm-2"
  ],
  "study_type": "In situ X-ray absorption spectroscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes proximal redox coupling as a general strategy for enhancing stability in atomically precise electrocatalysts through buffered redox responses during electrochemical operation."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines bidirectional neuromodulation technologies that enable both neural excitation and inhibition across multiple modalities. It compares electrical, optical, thermal, mechanical, magnetic, and chemical approaches in terms of spatiotemporal precision, penetration depth, specificity, and safety. The study highlights applications in neuroscience research and neurological disorder treatment while identifying technical challenges for future closed-loop adaptive systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Bidirectional neuromodulation technologies support both neural excitation and inhibition across diverse modalities",
    "Electrical, optical, thermal, mechanical, magnetic, and chemical approaches are compared for spatiotemporal performance and safety",
    "Applications include neuroscience research and neurological disorder treatment with potential for closed-loop adaptive regulation"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This review advances understanding of bidirectional neuromodulation by systematically comparing diverse technological approaches and their implications for precise neural regulation in both research and clinical contexts."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how human neutralizing autoantibodies against interferon-α (IFN-α) develop, mature, and function. By tracking plasma neutralization across multiple donors over years, researchers observed initial narrow specificity (often targeting IFN-α8) that broadened in breadth and potency. Monoclonal analyses showed affinity maturation generated subtype-restricted and broadly reactive antibodies. Structural studies revealed that antibodies engaging non-competing epitopes synergistically block IFN-α signaling and may trigger Fc-effector functions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Neutralizing autoantibodies against IFN-α initially target narrow specificities, often IFN-α8, but broaden over time.",
    "Affinity maturation produces both subtype-restricted and broadly reactive antibody classes.",
    "Antibodies engaging non-competing epitopes synergistically block IFN-α signaling and may trigger Fc-effector functions."
  ],
  "study_type": "Longitudinal human plasma tracking with monoclonal antibody analyses and structural characterization",
  "scientific_significance": "The study elucidates molecular mechanisms underlying the development and functional synergy of anti-IFN-α autoantibodies, revealing how specificity broadens to enhance pathogenic potential in autoimmune contexts."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated the evolutionary position of Middle Pleistocene Xujiayao hominins using proteomic analysis and morphological comparisons. Proteomic analysis identified 18 endogenous proteins in two specimens, and the Denisova-specific COL1A2-996 R>K mutation confirmed Denisovan affiliation. Morphological analysis revealed shared traits with other East Asian archaic Homo, indicating a Denisovan lineage with features intermediate between Homo erectus and modern humans.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Proteomic analysis of Xujiayao specimens identified 18 endogenous proteins",
    "Denisova-specific COL1A2-996 R>K mutation confirmed Denisovan affiliation",
    "Shared morphological traits with East Asian archaic Homo indicate Denisovan lineage"
  ],
  "study_type": "Proteomic analysis and morphological comparisons",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a comprehensive molecular and morphological characterization of Denisovan hominins, clarifying their evolutionary relationships and distinguishing features within the Middle Pleistocene hominin radiation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how intron retention in lung cancer affects STAT2 function and immunotherapy response. Using long-read RNA sequencing, researchers mapped alternative splicing in primary lung adenocarcinomas, identifying unannotated mRNA isoforms in immune-related genes. Retained introns in STAT2 altered protein isoforms, modulating immune signaling and interferon responses, and correlated with PD-L.1 immunotherapy outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Long-read RNA sequencing identified over 149,014 unannotated mRNA isoforms in lung adenocarcinomas, many in immune-related genes.",
    "Intron retention in STAT2 generated protein isoforms that regulate immune signaling and interferon responses.",
    "STAT2 intron retention correlates with patient survival after PD-L1 immunotherapy."
  ],
  "study_type": "Long-read RNA sequencing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes alternative splicing as a key regulator of tumor immune responses and identifies STAT2 intron retention as a predictive biomarker for immunotherapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates cathode depolarization's role in enhancing sodium deposition uniformity in anode-free sodium batteries (AFSBs). By establishing cathode-specific charging protocols that dynamically regulate current based on state-of-charge-dependent DC resistance, the research achieves record cyclability (1370 cycles at 2 C) and high energy density (180 Wh kg⁻¹) while demonstrating universal applicability across multiple cathode chemistries.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cathode-specific charging protocols dynamically adjust current based on state-of-charge-dependent DC resistance to depolarize cathodes",
    "AFSBs achieve 1370 cycles at 2 C and 180 Wh kg⁻¹ energy density with stable cycling",
    "The depolarization strategy works across diverse cathode chemistries including NFPP, NVP, and NFM"
  ],
  "study_type": "Experimental",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cathode depolarization as a critical mechanism for improving sodium deposition uniformity in anode-free sodium batteries, enabling longer cycle life and higher energy density without anodes."
}
